# interplay_dag_15: Order under concurrency, as dependency graphs (pgtest/dagwalk.py): GPU work spread over the root FIFO, up to 16 child FIFOs and several tasks, whose edges are barriers on other FIFOs' stamps and on the FIFO's own earlier ones. Each seed is one scenario, interplay_dag_<seed>; the shapes after them are written by hand.  Every node is one exec that leaves an order witness: it increments its chain's counter (inc_k) and copies the counter block out, runs add_one over its first predecessor's result, and copies each predecessor's first words into its slot. A chain's nodes are ordered by the graph, and a node reads only its ancestors, so every byte is fixed by the graph whatever the interleaving; a control run of every node, one after another in creation order on the root FIFO, is what every phase's slots and the chain counters are compared with. Also locked: the stamps every FIFO publishes after each phase and while work is held, the interrupt count of each phase (each exec publishes alone), the interrupts' order and snapshots where a phase is totally ordered, and the host's block calls, sorted and digested where FIFOs run at once.  What varies: width and depth, joins over several FIFOs (successive barriers), predecessors from earlier phases (their stamps already passed), work held behind the gate for 300 ms or for 2.5 s (past the blocked-barrier report) across nothing, a pause and unpause, or a suspend and a resume on a fresh device, child FIFOs deleted and defined again between phases (barriers on the old incarnation's stamps pass at once), up to three tasks over the same guest pages, opcodes 67 and 55.  What the implementation does not order, and so nothing here assumes: execs on one FIFO are ordered on the GPU only when they share a task. Two consecutive execs of different tasks on one FIFO, with no barrier between them, run at once (measured: the second, a copy of a counter the first increments 1100 times, read 0 in-process and in the service after the first round); in-process both tasks' buffers are different MTLBuffers over the same guest pages on the one exec queue, so Metal tracks no hazard between them, and in the service the second task's command buffer waits only for the FIFO event the first signals when it is scheduled. Their stamps still publish in order. So every edge here is a barrier, the one on the FIFO's previous node included.  This graph: children=6, tasks=1, window=100; 75 nodes in 4 phases (none 1, pause 1, pause_long 1, suspend 1), 13 chains, 40 joins, 7 FIFO deletes and defines.
